econet-eth: WHNAT: resolve the destination vif from the egress netdev

The flow bind picked the WHNAT vif index with a source-MAC lookup.  A routed
frame carries the MAC of the interface it leaves by, so when several AP vifs
share a bridge that is the bridge's MAC -- which Linux inherits from the
lowest-addressed port -- and every WiFi flow was force-to-CPU'd to whichever
radio sorted first.

Seen on an Archer XR500v with the 2.4 and 5 GHz APs in one bridge: the client
was associated to the 5 GHz vif, the bridge wore the 2.4 GHz MAC, and the
downstream was delivered to the 2.4 GHz BSS, which had no clients at all.  The
frames were lost on the air and TCP spent its time retransmitting; throughput
collapsed and connections stalled.  It is invisible without hardware offload,
because the bridge consults its FDB and gets the port right -- the engine
consults nothing and trusts the index encoded in the entry.

Use odev, the egress netdev already collected from FLOW_ACTION_REDIRECT and
already trusted by the ethernet path for its DSA port lookup.  The source-MAC
lookup stays as a fallback for flows that carry no redirect action.

--- a/econet_port.c
+++ b/econet_port.c
@@ -597,8 +597,22 @@
 	 * a registered mt76 AP vif): HALF-OFFLOAD -- force-to-CPU (dp=fcpu) so the
 	 * engine does the NAT then bounces the frame to the CPU, where the F3 demux
 	 * dev_queue_xmit's it to the mt76 vif (the OEM WHNAT model, cont32r/s).
-	 * PPPoE sid from FLOW_ACTION_PPPOE_PUSH (WiFi downstream has none). */
-	wifi_idx = en75_whnat_idx_by_mac(eth.h_source);
+	 * PPPoE sid from FLOW_ACTION_PPPOE_PUSH (WiFi downstream has none).
+	 *
+	 * Resolve the vif from the egress netdev, not from the source MAC. When
+	 * several AP vifs share a bridge, the routed frame carries the bridge's
+	 * MAC -- which Linux takes from the lowest-addressed port -- so a source
+	 * MAC lookup returns whichever radio happens to sort first, and the
+	 * engine then force-to-CPUs every frame to that BSS. Observed with the
+	 * 2.4 and 5 GHz APs bridged together: the client was on the 5 GHz vif,
+	 * the bridge wore the 2.4 GHz MAC, and the whole download was delivered
+	 * to the empty 2.4 GHz BSS. odev is the physical egress port (the same
+	 * value the ethernet path feeds to dsa_user_dev_check() below), so it
+	 * names the BSS directly. Keep the MAC lookup as a fallback for the
+	 * unbridged case, where the flow may carry no redirect action. */
+	wifi_idx = en75_whnat_idx_by_dev(odev);
+	if (wifi_idx < 0)
+		wifi_idx = en75_whnat_idx_by_mac(eth.h_source);
 	en75_foe_entry_prepare(&e, MTK_PPE_PKT_TYPE_IPV4_HNAPT, l4proto,
 			       wifi_idx >= 0 ? READ_ONCE(en75_ppe_fcpu_dp) : 1,
 			       eth.h_source, eth.h_dest);
